{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789754244,"nonce":"1rGnIDALshCQWedP89w2vYLWq6Q=","title":"Windows: the host runtime on windows-2022","message":"The runtime compiled for Windows before but its non-unix branches were stubs: no conservative stack ranges (thread bounds (0, 0)), no safepoint page, and the wren_call_N trampolines are SysV assembly. Landed: VirtualAlloc reservations for the Immix heap and the safepoint page, VirtualProtect for the stop, a vectored exception handler that parks the faulting thread from its CONTEXT, GetCurrentThreadStackLimits for the scan, rsi/rdi in the register spill, and the plain trampolines on x86_64 Windows. CI runs the test and LLVM jobs on windows-2022 through the llvm21 action (micromamba llvmdev, as Ash).\n\nVerified so far by cargo clippy --target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc --all-targets from macOS (a stub C compiler stands in for the C deps' build scripts). Runtime behaviour is what the runner reports.\n\nLeft:\n- AOT on Windows: link_executable drives cc with unix flags; the site's Windows page promises wlift --aot to .exe.\n- krio stacks on Windows are heap-allocated without a guard page.\n- aarch64-pc-windows is not claimed (krio does not switch there).\n- The gc ring buffer stays unix-only (diagnostic).","files":null}]}